
Perform an exhaustive code review on bocl, a Common Lisp implementation.

Read the "Introduction to Bootstrap Common Lisp -- BOCL."
in `documentation.org`.


1- code review of the kernel written in C.

Very important: this code should never crash.

Also it should run on a variety of target platforms, compiled with any
C17 standard compiler, preferably without any POSIX target platform
specific idiosyncrasie or API, but using just the C17 standard
library. (The architecture may vary, byte order, word length, etc).

So it's very important to check all undefined behavior of the C code,
and correct them to ensure we stay in defined behavior territory and
avoid any crash situation (invalid memory accesses, invalid/overflow
in arithmetic operations, etc).

The kernel code written in C should also ensure that the invariants
required by the lisp system are always fulfilled, notably concerning
the memory management and garbage collector, and concerning the type
systems and lisp object integrity.  The error reporting and handling
thru the Common Lisp condition system should be especially validated.

Common Lisp functions may be implemented entirely in C, in which case,
they should also implement a conforming behavior wit respect to the
Common Lisp standard.  But it is also possible that the actual public
lisp function is implemented in the Common Lisp layer above the
kernel, in which case the C version may be more lax (as long as the 
Common Lisp wrapper ensure conformance.


2- code review of the Common Lisp layer written in Common Lisp.  The
rest of the Common Lisp implementation is written in Common Lisp,
above the C kernel layer. It should be validated, notably its
conformance with respect to the Common Lisp standard, but absence of
bugs and error, notably when processing limits or uncommon cases.
 
The varions Common Lisp declaring limits of the implementation should
be checked and validated, to ensure that a program relying on them can
work conformingly without encountering problems.


3- Some Common Lisp functions may or have to have implementation
specific behaviors, as specified by the Common Lisp standard.  Verify
that such implementation specific behavior is not gratuituous, and
that it is correctly documented in the section
"Implementation-Dependent Features" of `documentation.org`


4- Extensions: bocl will provide a few extensions as documented.
Verify that they're correctly docummented in the section
"Extensions to Common Lisp as specified by ANSI INCITS 226-1994 (S20018)"
of `documentation.org`


5- validation of the results of the ansi-tests. Once the ansi-tests
can run, their results must be verified and validated, and may signal
conformance problems or bugs.

Code review reports should be written in this code-review/ directory.
